proofs.proof_tex:
Write
\[
I_{a,s}=\mathbf 1\{G=a,S=s\},\qquad
p_{a,s}(x)=P(G=a,S=s\mid X=x),\qquad
\pi=P(G=g,S=1)=E_P[p_{g,1}(X)],
\]
and set \(\mu_{a,s}(x)=E_P[D\mid G=a,S=s,X=x]\).  In the one-period target, the definitions of \(q\) and \(\theta\) reduce to
\[
q(x)=\mu_{g,1}(x)-\mu_{g,0}(x)-\mu_{c,1}(x)+\mu_{c,0}(x),
\qquad
\theta=\frac{E_P[p_{g,1}(X)q(X)]}{\pi}.
\tag{R1}
\]
Retained-cell overlap gives \(p_{a,s}(X)\ge\varepsilon\) almost surely for the four retained cells and
\[
\pi=E_P[p_{g,1}(X)]\ge\varepsilon>0,
\tag{R2}
\]
so every ratio below is well defined.  Definition \(\ref{def:pairwise-score}\), with \(D=\Delta Y_g\), gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\Psi_{k,c,P}
=\frac1\pi\bigg[&I_{g,1}\{q(X)-\theta+D-\mu_{g,1}(X)\}
-I_{g,0}\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{g,0}(X)}\{D-\mu_{g,0}(X)\}\\
&-I_{c,1}\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{c,1}(X)}\{D-\mu_{c,1}(X)\}
+I_{c,0}\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{c,0}(X)}\{D-\mu_{c,0}(X)\}\bigg].
\end{aligned}
\tag{R3}
\]

For the Ortiz--Villavicencio--Sant'Anna reduction, relabel their target cohort \(2\) as \(g\), their never-enabling cohort \(\infty\) as \(c\), and their eligibility indicator as \(S\).  Lemma \(\ref{lem:causal-identification}\) identifies their \(\operatorname{ATT}(2,2)\) with \(\theta\).  For every retained cell \((a,s)\), iterated expectation and (R2) give
\[
\begin{aligned}
E_P\!\left[I_{a,s}\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{a,s}(X)}\right]
&=E_P\!\left[E_P[I_{a,s}\mid X]
\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{a,s}(X)}\right]\\
&=E_P[p_{g,1}(X)]=\pi.
\end{aligned}
\tag{R4}
\]
Thus the cited target weight is \(I_{g,1}/\pi\), and every cited comparison weight is
\[
\frac{I_{a,s}p_{g,1}(X)}{\pi p_{a,s}(X)}.
\tag{R5}
\]
The target pseudo-outcome in the cited formula satisfies, by (R1),
\[
\begin{aligned}
&D-\mu_{g,0}(X)-\mu_{c,1}(X)+\mu_{c,0}(X)-\theta\\
&\qquad=D-\mu_{g,1}(X)+q(X)-\theta.
\end{aligned}
\tag{R6}
\]
Substitution of (R4)--(R6) into the formula transcribed in Lemma \(\ref{lem:ovs-two-period-panel-ddd-eif}\) gives exactly the four terms of (R3), in the cell order \((g,1),(g,0),(c,1),(c,0)\) and with signs \(+,-,-,+\).  Hence the OVS efficient influence function equals \(\Psi_{k,c,P}\) term by term.

For the Akbari--Kiyavash--Ghassami reduction, relabel \(G_A=1\) as \(G=g\), \(G_A=0\) as \(G=c\), and \(D_A\) as \(S\).  Their target becomes, by (R1) and conditional expectation,
\[
\psi_{\mathrm{pd}}
=E_P[q(X)\mid G=g,S=1]
=\frac{E_P[p_{g,1}(X)q(X)]}{\pi}
=\theta.
\tag{R7}
\]
Under the same relabeling, \(G_AD_A=I_{g,1}\) and
\(\pi_{r,a,s}(X)=p_{g,1}(X)/p_{a,s}(X)\).  The formula transcribed in Lemma \(\ref{lem:akg-panel-ddd-eif}\) is therefore
\[
\eta_A
=\frac1\pi\sum_{a,s\in\{0,1\}}\sigma_{a,s}
\left\{I_{g,1}-I_{a,s}\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{a,s}(X)}\right\}
\{D-\mu_{a,s}(X)\}
-\frac{I_{g,1}}\pi\theta,
\tag{R8}
\]
where \(a=1\) denotes \(g\), \(a=0\) denotes \(c\), and \(\sigma_{a,s}=(-1)^{a+s+1}\).  In the order \((0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1)\),
\[
(\sigma_{0,0},\sigma_{0,1},\sigma_{1,0},\sigma_{1,1})=(-1,+1,+1,-1),
\qquad \sum_{a,s}\sigma_{a,s}=0.
\tag{R9}
\]
The part of (R8) carrying the common indicator is consequently
\[
\begin{aligned}
I_{g,1}\sum_{a,s}\sigma_{a,s}\{D-\mu_{a,s}(X)\}
&=-I_{g,1}\sum_{a,s}\sigma_{a,s}\mu_{a,s}(X)\\
&=I_{g,1}q(X),
\end{aligned}
\tag{R10}
\]
where the second equality is (R1).  The cell-specific part has coefficients
\[
-\sigma_{a,s}=(+1,-1,-1,+1)
\tag{R11}
\]
in the same order.  In particular, for \((a,s)=(1,1)\) its propensity ratio equals one.  Combining (R7)--(R11) gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\eta_A=\frac1\pi\bigg[&I_{g,1}\{q(X)-\theta+D-\mu_{g,1}(X)\}
-I_{g,0}\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{g,0}(X)}\{D-\mu_{g,0}(X)\}\\
&-I_{c,1}\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{c,1}(X)}\{D-\mu_{c,1}(X)\}
+I_{c,0}\frac{p_{g,1}(X)}{p_{c,0}(X)}\{D-\mu_{c,0}(X)\}\bigg]
=\Psi_{k,c,P}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{R12}
\]
This proves the second term-by-term equality, including the denominator derivative \(-I_{g,1}\theta/\pi\).

In the stated two-period, two-cohort design, the clean comparison set at the sole post-treatment period contains only \(c\).  There are no two distinct clean cohorts and hence no nonzero row in \(\mathcal E_{\mathrm{OVS}}\).  The zero-dimensional convention in Definition \(\ref{def:ovs-normal-projection}\) therefore gives
\[
Z_P^{\mathrm{OVS}}=0,
\qquad p_{\mathrm{OVS},P}=0.
\tag{R13}
\]
With one target and \(\omega_k=1\), the definition of the reference gradient gives \(\psi_P^0=\Psi_{k,c,P}\).  The definitions of \(\phi_{\mathrm{OVS},P}\), together with (R13), imply for every such law
\[
\phi_{\mathrm{OVS},P}=\psi_P^0=\Psi_{k,c,P}.
\tag{R14}
\]
At the fixed-finite-support conditional-mean-interior laws covered by Theorem \(\ref{thm:complete-normal-canonical-gradient}\), that theorem proves tangent exhaustion and therefore proves that the common expression in (R14) is the canonical gradient in the present published observed-law model.  This finite-support qualification is essential: the theorem makes no continuous-covariate tangent-exhaustion claim.  If \(\omega_k\) is an arbitrary scalar, linearity of the target pathwise derivative and the definitions of \(\psi_P^0\) and \(\phi_{\mathrm{OVS},P}\) multiply all three expressions by \(\omega_k\).

Finally, Lemma \(\ref{lem:csx-staggered-did-eif-scope}\) supplies a distinct conditional-efficiency formula for a staggered-DiD target under its stated assumptions.  None of (R1)--(R14) constructs an embedding or equality between that staggered-DiD tangent space and the present eligibility-gap DDD tangent space.  The result proved here is therefore exactly the two panel-DDD reductions above and entails no nesting claim concerning the Chen--Sant'Anna--Xie model.

Fix \(e=(u,c,c')\in\mathcal E_{\mathrm{OVS}}\), and write
\[
\sigma_{e,c,1}=1,\qquad \sigma_{e,c,0}=-1,\qquad
\sigma_{e,c',1}=-1,\qquad \sigma_{e,c',0}=1.
\]
All conditional expectations below exist by the integrability in the nuisance and signal definitions and the displayed lower bound on the candidate propensities.  For one of the four cells \((a,s)\), Definition \(\ref{def:orthogonal-ovs-normal-signal}\), the definition of \(p_{a,s}\), and the definition of \(m_{a,s,u}\) give
\[
\begin{aligned}
&E_P\!\left[\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)
+\frac{\mathbf 1\{G=a,S=s\}}{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}
 \{\Delta Y_u-\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)\}\,\middle|\,X\right]\\
&\qquad=\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)
+\frac{p_{a,s}(X)}{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}
 \{m_{a,s,u}(X)-\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)\}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{35a}
\]
Subtracting \(m_{a,s,u}(X)\) from the last expression and collecting terms yields the exact identity
\[
\widetilde m_{a,s,u}-m_{a,s,u}
+\frac{p_{a,s}}{\widetilde p_{a,s}}(m_{a,s,u}-\widetilde m_{a,s,u})
=\frac{\widetilde p_{a,s}-p_{a,s}}{\widetilde p_{a,s}}
 (\widetilde m_{a,s,u}-m_{a,s,u}).
\tag{35b}
\]
The signal is the signed sum of these four augmented cell means.  Multiplying (35b) by the declared signs and summing proves
\[
E_P[S_e^{\mathrm{OVS}}(O;\widetilde\eta)\mid X]-g_e^{\mathrm{OVS}}(X)
=\sum_{(a,s)\in\{(c,1),(c,0),(c',1),(c',0)\}}
\sigma_{e,a,s}
\frac{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)-p_{a,s}(X)}{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}
\{\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)-m_{a,s,u}(X)\},
\]
which is (35).  Along a one-dimensional nuisance path through the truth, the right side has no term linear in a propensity perturbation with the regression fixed at its truth, and no term linear in a regression perturbation with the propensity fixed at its truth.  More generally, along a joint differentiable path both factors are of order equal to the path parameter, so their product is second order.  This proves the two Gateaux derivatives are zero.

By Theorem \(\ref{thm:observable-equivalence}\), a law induced by the published OVS model obeys \(d_{c,u}(X)=d_{c',u}(X)\) for every retained OVS row.  Hence \(g_e^{\mathrm{OVS}}(X)=0\) almost surely.  Substitution of \(\widetilde\eta=(p,m)\) into Definition \(\ref{def:orthogonal-ovs-normal-signal}\) then gives
\[
S_e^{\mathrm{OVS}}(O;\eta)=Z_{e,P}^{\mathrm{OVS}}(O)
\quad\text{almost surely}.
\]
Finally, the assumed clipping gives \(\widetilde p_{a,s}\ge\varepsilon/2\), and therefore
\[
\left\|
\frac{\widetilde p_{a,s}-p_{a,s}}{\widetilde p_{a,s}}
(\widetilde m_{a,s,u}-m_{a,s,u})
\right\|_{P_X,2}
\le \frac{2}{\varepsilon}
\|\widetilde p_{a,s}-p_{a,s}\|_{P_X,4}
\|\widetilde m_{a,s,u}-m_{a,s,u}\|_{P_X,4}
\tag{35c}
\]
by H\"older's inequality.  There are four terms per row and a fixed finite number of rows because \(T\) and \(\mathcal G\) are fixed.  Applying the triangle inequality to (35c), and using the finite-dimensional norms declared for \(p\) and \(m\), gives (36) with a constant \(C_\varepsilon\) depending only on \(\varepsilon\) and the fixed number of coordinates.

Fix \(e=(u,c,c')\in\mathcal E_{\mathrm{per}}\).  Introduce the four signed cell indices
\[
\mathcal A_e=\{(c,1),(c,0),(c',1),(c',0)\},\qquad
\sigma_{c,1}=1,\quad \sigma_{c,0}=-1,\quad
\sigma_{c',1}=-1,\quad \sigma_{c',0}=1.
\]
For each \((a,s)\in\mathcal A_e\), the definitions of \(p_{a,s}\) and \(m_{a,s,u}\), iterated conditioning, and positivity of \(\widetilde p_{a,s}\) give
\[
\begin{aligned}
&E_P\!\left[
 \frac{\mathbf 1_{a,s}}{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}
 \{\Delta Y_u-\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)\}
 \mathrel{\Big|}X\right]\\
&\qquad=
\frac{1}{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}
 E_P\!\left[
 \mathbf 1_{a,s}
 E_P\{\Delta Y_u-\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)\mid G,S,X\}
 \mathrel{\Big|}X\right]\\
&\qquad=
\frac{p_{a,s}(X)}{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}
\{m_{a,s,u}(X)-\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)\}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{1}
\]
All divisions in (1) are legitimate because the premise imposes
\(\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)\geq\varepsilon/2>0\) almost surely.

By Definition \(\ref{def:orthogonal-complete-normal-signal}\), the candidate restriction level and its true counterpart are
\[
\widetilde g_e(X)=\sum_{(a,s)\in\mathcal A_e}
 \sigma_{a,s}\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X),
\qquad
g_e(X)=\sum_{(a,s)\in\mathcal A_e}
 \sigma_{a,s}m_{a,s,u}(X).
\]
Summing (1) with the signs \(\sigma_{a,s}\), adding
\(\widetilde g_e(X)-g_e(X)\), and using the definition of \(S_e\) yields the exact conditional-drift identity
\[
\begin{aligned}
E_P[S_e(O;\widetilde\eta)\mid X]-g_e(X)
&=\sum_{(a,s)\in\mathcal A_e}\sigma_{a,s}
 \{\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)-m_{a,s,u}(X)\}
 \left\{1-\frac{p_{a,s}(X)}{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}\right\}\\
&=\sum_{(a,s)\in\mathcal A_e}\sigma_{a,s}
 \frac{\{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)-p_{a,s}(X)\}
       \{\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)-m_{a,s,u}(X)\}}
      {\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{2}
\]
Thus the drift is exactly a finite sum of products of a propensity error and an outcome-regression error.  In particular, if only one nuisance is varied from the truth while the other is held at its true value, (2) is identically zero.  More generally, along a Gateaux path
\(\widetilde p_h=p+h\dot p+o(h)\) and
\(\widetilde m_h=m+h\dot m+o(h)\) in the displayed \(L_4(P_X)\) norms, (2) is \(O(h^2)\) in \(L_2(P_X)\).  Its derivative at \(h=0\) is therefore zero, proving Neyman orthogonality with respect to either nuisance at the truth.

The clipping premise gives
\(\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)^{-1}\leq 2/\varepsilon\) almost surely.  Hence the triangle inequality applied to (2), followed by H\"older's inequality with exponents \(4,4,2\), gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\|E_P[S_e(O;\widetilde\eta)\mid X]-g_e(X)\|_{P_X,2}
&\leq \frac{2}{\varepsilon}
 \sum_{(a,s)\in\mathcal A_e}
 \|\widetilde p_{a,s}-p_{a,s}\|_{P_X,4}
 \|\widetilde m_{a,s,u}-m_{a,s,u}\|_{P_X,4}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{3}
\]
The coordinate set \(\mathcal E_{\mathrm{per}}\), the retained cell set, and the period set are finite.  Therefore, summing the squared coordinate bounds in (3) and using equivalence of norms in the resulting fixed finite-dimensional spaces produces a finite constant \(C_\varepsilon\), depending only on \(\varepsilon\) and those fixed index sets, such that
\[
\|E_P[S^{\mathrm{per}}(O;\widetilde\eta)\mid X]-g(X)\|_{P_X,2}
\leq C_\varepsilon
\|\widetilde p-p\|_{P_X,4}
\|\widetilde m-m\|_{P_X,4}.
\tag{4}
\]
This argument also covers the zero-dimensional convention, in which both sides of (4) are zero.

Finally, substituting the true nuisance \(\eta=(p,m)\) into Definition \(\ref{def:orthogonal-complete-normal-signal}\) gives
\[
S_e(O;\eta)=g_e(X)+Z^{\mathrm{per}}_{e,P}(O).
\tag{5}
\]
Every \(e\in\mathcal E_{\mathrm{per}}\) is, by Definition \(\ref{def:complete-normal-projection}\), a localized observable period-specific equality row.  Under those equalities, Definition \(\ref{def:local-observed-model}\), consistently with Theorem \(\ref{thm:observable-equivalence}\), gives \(g_e(X)=0\) \(P_X\)-almost surely.  Equation (5) consequently yields
\(S_e(O;\eta)=Z^{\mathrm{per}}_{e,P}(O)\) almost surely for every retained row, and hence
\(S^{\mathrm{per}}(O;\eta)=Z_P^{\mathrm{per}}(O)\) almost surely.  This proves all assertions.
